#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/id #uuid "122fd33e-5ef4-5200-9c72-67f40f3b8ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8168" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/e51c52e07d49052f578138e5c1d7e9f84a95ba8c454e7fcf834dcc9047dad432.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2.$  Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}.$\n\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d782c826-1111-5c83-98b6-220856d74b41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8169" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/791b214858b7ff88b6ad1ba98ea8336abbd678ffb34751b041e11cec8cd44143.png)\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/b7df527ba563debac29dbdd838aadb4f219653a77077b70b65bdbfe579e26a0a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/1a8e02bec4af0b88d7fa0f4cd87b9e0f89757ca87f494e1451650ac4f3a06dcf.png) into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 23\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =86.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Quadratic Form Given Its Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f3be44df-f059-5e92-b279-644fe1eb0985" :question/math-academy-id "e-8170" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/59d795aef27f3ae7598e1a8503ba0dbde377486b355e0db6decc4753f9ae7544.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/31a4a301bddfd7435f188f76c1702beb7c2eea01a1b174d5195f321cfdef3103.png)" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term $a_{ij}x_i x_j$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =-1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(1){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(1){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+-1{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161711 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9ed75397-5920-5326-a5b9-7537e30f0a85" :question/math-academy-id "e-8171" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/1bd9859db9b628b26d250310d38222e8d1474cbfa8d5d991217dc74d04e3f47d.png)\nThe matrix of the quadratic form ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/d4ecb15785c7b91e8596dd5ee6d5f4f37bf8021c5be668ec618955d401d4818f.png) is given above. What is the value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/5076087e153b1b98d9b1798601bd9f4fb4858085d9fd7b5695ee28d0ec82383c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/8645c292302b7edfc416cf5ca509325a6ee9381fb231a7f2b159803b4dfda807.png)\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/dfb385bea2635645b1f9c86af71eff0dce97a954417745dbd38490f018f08e0a.png) between the entries ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/6a348fce74db828ec978c7e135e99593bc998bfabecec36ecaf759cc06dba537.png) and\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/0ce406288d4b63c3156067f4ff753a5fec13d4461f11cf58252a1361f16b6911.png) between the entries ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/c24c0fe76b0f877030d6130109d0b82ef187e05be9c103d8b913b9c3eb654200.png)\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/e8fda0676083681fa61f11e26ca5455e06cf93ce11b657f7e1b46be3c30ff899.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/29e111e47c4fb83d1a5be9b51f83e463770c5c5d7a99eae453281f617e34fe86.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/d6faa78e35bd56fb2a138698ca72569d54c7da3305c3bb5b9dfafd8662d49d7a.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/a8c65541492f0454624e520d1b86952208a1206735718dd49b7455f003d2bce5.png) Finally, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/851f6d6822dca779fa17406a68c7e6fbce37cf2642ce5994f8e95ede41e31921.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4809d6b8-54c2-5c32-a52e-c040650c79a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329462} {:answer/id #uuid "00a531bf-e7a8-5a50-8c7f-fd27d0459d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329463} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1f5581-f82a-568c-b69d-7cddc35596a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329464} {:answer/id #uuid "56bf286f-55b5-5d08-b06f-f523de6e25ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329465} {:answer/id #uuid "088302bb-c647-5b28-9c14-bffb3a522f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4809d6b8-54c2-5c32-a52e-c040650c79a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329462} :answer-field/id #uuid "1054e6d5-cbf9-530b-89b1-b4ff3d2dcf8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0963d160-f5ae-51a3-8391-bc24ece6fe93" :question/math-academy-id "q-140870" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Quadratic Form Given Its Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8cbfee-e53b-501e-a2a3-25444ae58ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329469} {:answer/id #uuid "924b1430-70aa-58a7-a5ce-247012855de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329470} {:answer/id #uuid "309d74c0-5669-5797-97b3-75efbfec634a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329471} {:answer/id #uuid "48f7ecd5-10d3-54f2-af5e-cc25baf11f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329472} {:answer/id #uuid "870b3125-b82d-5dd0-b2bf-a1d4b061049f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "870b3125-b82d-5dd0-b2bf-a1d4b061049f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329473} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f75f9c0-687a-5392-9af8-c1d37e52ce39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a4aa31a-9bd3-531e-9d58-38401292e334" :question/math-academy-id "q-140928" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-1){x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+(-1){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-{x}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34bd976-257d-5945-8a50-eceb2a407ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329476} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bbc316-0329-531a-98a4-1a3e1f8a28c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329477} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc39b96-a204-55cf-ac76-e3eb2a9b4929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329478} {:answer/id #uuid "3979a24d-dd44-5002-a16a-2afe4b43c2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329479} {:answer/id #uuid "56d51bb0-20f2-57c0-a14e-dc4c879af00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3979a24d-dd44-5002-a16a-2afe4b43c2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329479} :answer-field/id #uuid "dedf6fd6-47a3-5036-97a0-506ab279bd4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46502b93-4d5f-5b44-a9ca-d570ed1198d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-140932" :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=2+(-2)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d30c4f25-ad01-5e24-8648-cd478bf76be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329482} {:answer/id #uuid "61aa4b7e-2e12-5a51-b4c8-ea0b4d616fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329483} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9a4534-ef46-54ed-ab3c-5ff1b11bf4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329484} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc5e4e0-e3c6-5c9b-b93b-827b7364fa16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329485} {:answer/id #uuid "37c2ac69-1dd2-59dc-9509-5e16d930d734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc5e4e0-e3c6-5c9b-b93b-827b7364fa16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329485} :answer-field/id #uuid "a65dd035-8267-5c8c-bbc7-5b94fc3f96dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ec77bfc-fd0e-5f6f-9e51-81a56de42ba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93700" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $Q$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $R$ is not symmetric since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c030edd3-d9de-5b53-b6de-a14d7b445742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160747} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bf1ce9-9b18-59fc-a92e-d8d79c618548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160748} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f7ee18-5a83-5353-9a44-05841ae48130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160749} {:answer/id #uuid "03010865-79b8-5e50-9098-dd40a819f3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160750} {:answer/id #uuid "53ea4e1c-68fd-525b-b350-12a97fd3e6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03010865-79b8-5e50-9098-dd40a819f3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160750} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5ed7ae-7ac7-5043-814d-c93fd8266116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b88a995-140a-54a3-a483-210a7791965f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93703" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-4)+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+0+16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b038bef1-8372-5f4e-9391-3983c4d37896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329489} {:answer/id #uuid "80d827be-2fe4-56ba-88ee-ce2118618c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329490} {:answer/id #uuid "f68ddd99-8e93-58a0-8703-1a829823bb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329491} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd11c93-da7f-50ca-a99c-447ff639f3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329492} {:answer/id #uuid "ad828e1c-0a52-5070-bd73-408501ff526a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b038bef1-8372-5f4e-9391-3983c4d37896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329489} :answer-field/id #uuid "42498f08-664b-537c-96d9-4bf11aa4556a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76222307-a5b8-5a1d-9d57-08bb293d8059" :question/math-academy-id "q-94059" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $K$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $L$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since ${m}_{12}\\ne {m}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$K$ and $L$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431f5dc9-43fd-5659-9be5-d0d3fc111555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329496} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9c981b-70e3-5d8a-9377-573cd72a6735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329497} {:answer/id #uuid "80ba4e4f-28c6-5d41-a070-dc945f834227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329498} {:answer/id #uuid "29265b6a-dba7-5edf-ab60-e9cfca4f8762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329499} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7573b4-df12-5cce-a188-4195e7665a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29265b6a-dba7-5edf-ab60-e9cfca4f8762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329499} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce098629-fda8-5827-9e69-6ff00950b170" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a59cc2a-02c1-5501-be18-04bcc0a03c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-94062" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $U$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $V$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ and $U$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e75160-4372-5a2e-862c-540c97f229a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329503} {:answer/id #uuid "259e4baa-b429-5e75-9ff2-0e9c00f26ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329504} {:answer/id #uuid "c557f540-6a99-56b2-93fc-ef96b910ee2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329505} {:answer/id #uuid "587071b7-73b8-5ff7-b25a-1803557bbf24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329506} {:answer/id #uuid "be82801d-6d79-5b21-bd7f-c66c8e2ea35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ and $F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c557f540-6a99-56b2-93fc-ef96b910ee2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329505} :answer-field/id #uuid "5442bd65-48e9-5ebd-a85f-3791b0368e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0af9db6-5918-5172-a76f-e795c725976c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94063" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ has an associated $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $D$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}.$ Although it's a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $D$ is not symmetric since ${d}_{24}\\ne {d}_{42}.$\n\n- $E$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $4\\times 4$).\n\n- $F$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it is a $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$F$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3491a842-78d4-5bf4-87cf-0ae7ff36be4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329510} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1b129d-7f3c-5973-ae02-0c76d2d8b8c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329511} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3a7cb3-0613-5182-be9b-51807c8a0aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329512} {:answer/id #uuid "7f830fb4-c3f2-5ce8-95de-cd5f0a0f2150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329513} {:answer/id #uuid "a540d716-5738-56d0-a7d0-3f13ad0ccad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f830fb4-c3f2-5ce8-95de-cd5f0a0f2150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329513} :answer-field/id #uuid "6afb0a8f-9e2c-596f-8083-e6f38f6622d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60610a64-9519-52ff-a828-fa5ed72def71" :question/math-academy-id "q-94068" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}+6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fd8c3f-18b8-5082-a427-3a12dfab84d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329517} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3fd1b6-a90c-5cca-87ac-ac15d70e1d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329518} {:answer/id #uuid "df450504-41ff-5a56-a810-a5d370b7034f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329519} {:answer/id #uuid "e76025df-1c8d-528b-a2f5-9b22c5b2d32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329520} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2e7b66-996b-51d5-bf1e-d91db7352f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01fd8c3f-18b8-5082-a427-3a12dfab84d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329517} :answer-field/id #uuid "2192c36e-8c3c-5727-8a39-62473541af2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3d5dabb-0455-5cd7-98c1-aa588dfa7596" :question/math-academy-id "q-94069" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)+(-2)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0+4+0-0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91cdd038-19c9-5d17-8cf4-a7ed07e565e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329524} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef3d559-08f5-5bb6-bde6-95b7cefa5705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329525} {:answer/id #uuid "15dd36b5-0cbe-5b23-b001-a0b53127c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329526} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a61166-3797-5460-8c56-46d77cacfd89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329527} {:answer/id #uuid "a0987f74-3140-5562-8061-a25b731c989b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15dd36b5-0cbe-5b23-b001-a0b53127c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329526} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3bd68df-2125-5ea7-83c3-c4d3505e1836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "471337b8-f399-528b-a6f9-2aa14b02c489" :question/math-academy-id "q-94086" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ddfe168-f394-500f-8d06-64f7491338ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329531} {:answer/id #uuid "f3be278e-51e5-547e-a6b0-48c0ab5c4aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329532} {:answer/id #uuid "fc23782d-feb4-517b-afae-108b72298938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329533} {:answer/id #uuid "ce66e990-4984-5b7c-ab66-d3665ecdc061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329534} {:answer/id #uuid "7df437cb-7ab5-5d04-abec-f4a4e0623be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ddfe168-f394-500f-8d06-